window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][74] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Many users of a social app say they spend more time on it than they want. How would you improve the product?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a screen-time reminder,&quot; with no view of the conflict with the business or of who is affected.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the tension between time spent and user wellbeing, chooses a segment, offers different moves, and sets metrics that allow time spent to fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Agrees in advance how much engagement the company will trade, protects younger users with defaults, applies Goodhart's law, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll start with the tension, since it shapes the answer. I'll assume the product earns revenue from advertising, so time spent matters commercially (an assumption I'd confirm), and that users are telling us the product sometimes works against their own goals. That is a goal conflict, and the interviewer will want to see how I handle it, not how I avoid it. <em>State.</em> Role: a core product and revenue engine. Users: people who say they spend more than they want, which I'd define by a short survey and by behavior (late-night sessions, long unplanned sessions), not only by volume; people who spend a lot and are content; and people who use it briefly. Funnel: open, scroll, interact, close. A feed that does not end has no natural stopping point. <em>Area.</em> I choose adult users who report that they spend more time than they want, at the moment of a long unplanned session. Their stated regret is a direct signal, their number is large, and the product's design contributes to the problem. I treat younger users separately with stricter defaults, because they are more vulnerable, and I'd involve safety and legal teams from the start; rules differ by country <code>[V: rules differ by country, for example India's DPDP Act treats anyone under 18 as a child]</code>. <em>Problem.</em> The feed has no natural end. People do not notice time passing, and the product offers no moment to decide to stop. Workarounds: deleting the app and reinstalling it, using the phone's own limits, and logging out. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: make the existing time tools easier to find and use. Add: a &quot;you're all caught up&quot; point after new content ends, and an optional reminder after a session length the person chooses. Remove: the next-item autoplay after the caught-up point. Reposition: from maximizing time to maximizing value, a change in what the product optimizes. Public accounts of one such shift exist: in early 2018 Facebook's chief executive said a change to the News Feed would reduce time spent in favor of more meaningful interactions, and the company reported a drop in time spent <code>Public</code> <code>[V: CS-C05-02, Facebook's January 2018 post and call]</code> (C05, CS-C05-02). I recommend the Add and the Fix first, with the Reposition as the longer question. <em>Metrics, and the trade.</em> Primary: the share of surveyed users in the segment who say they are satisfied with how they spend time on the product. That is a self-report and a proxy, and I'd pair it with behavior. Guardrails: long-run retention (a product that is healthier to use should keep people over years) and the reach of creators, who depend on attention. Counter metric: time spent and sessions per user, which I expect to fall, and I'd agree before launch how much of a fall is acceptable (say no more than 3% in the first quarter, invented), so that the team and leadership decide the trade in advance and not under pressure. Goodhart's law applies: if satisfaction became a target, it could be raised by changing how the survey is asked, so I'd keep the question fixed and audit it. <em>Risks.</em> Winners and losers: users in the segment gain; creators who depend on long sessions lose reach; advertisers see fewer impressions. Defaults have a large effect on what people end up with (<code>MM-defaults</code>, C07), so for younger users the reminders and limits are on by default. I'd reverse the break point if retention among the segment fell without a gain in reported satisfaction. I'd change my plan if the survey showed that the regret came mostly from one feature, and a targeted change would do more than a general break. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that improvement prompts at Meta can pair engagement questions with wellbeing and integrity tradeoffs <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or internal decision is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An enterprise software product has a legacy report feature used by 4% of customers that consumes 20% of the team's maintenance effort. The team wants to remove it. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Remove it, since few customers use it,&quot; or &quot;keep it, since customers pay for it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who the 4% are and what they pay, applies the fence test, compares options and sets a migration path.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies revenue at risk with and without a path, chooses a path that gives customers time and a replacement, defines how success and harm will be measured, and says when to stop.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat this as a removal decision with legacy users, and the number 4% is the start of the questions, not the answer. First the fence test (FW-C08-05): who is in the 4%, what do they pay, why do they use it, and why was it built? A small share of customers can be a large share of revenue, and enterprise customers often build routines around a report. <em>Assumptions.</em> 5,000 customers (invented), so 200 use the feature. They average $30,000 a year (invented), so $6,000,000 a year is at stake. The 20% of maintenance effort equals two engineer-years on a team of ten (invented), at $200,000 each fully loaded, so $400,000 a year. <em>Options.</em> | Option | Revenue at risk | Cost and effect | |---|---|---| | A. Remove it now | 15% churn among the 200 (invented): $900,000 | Frees $400,000 a year; breaks routines; damages trust | | B. Keep it and freeze it | Near zero | Saves part of the effort; the debt stays | | C. Replace it with a better report and migrate users over 12 months | 3% churn (invented): $180,000 | Frees $400,000 a year after the transition; costs migration work | | D. Keep it as it is | Zero | Keeps all the cost and the opportunity cost of two engineers | The arithmetic: 15% of $6,000,000 is $900,000, more than the $400,000 saved, so an abrupt removal loses money in the first year. With a path, 3% of $6,000,000 is $180,000, less than the saving, and the saving then continues. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend C: build the replacement, announce the change early, migrate the data and reports automatically, keep the old feature running for 12 months, and then remove it. It gives up the immediate saving. First I'd talk to a sample of the 200, especially the largest, to learn why they use it, and the replacement should meet that need and not just the feature's description. I'd check contracts and any commitment that names the feature. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of the 200 accounts migrated by the deadline. Guardrails: churn and contraction among the 200 compared with other accounts, and support tickets. Success: maintenance effort actually freed, and what it is used for; if it goes to the roadmap, I'd say what shipped. Counter metric: sentiment among affected accounts. <em>Risks and stopping rule.</em> The loudest accounts may be the strategic ones, so I'd treat each large account individually. I'd stop the removal, or extend the date, if migration is below a threshold set in advance (say 70% by month nine, invented) or if a strategic account says it will leave. I'd change the plan entirely if the interviews showed the feature does something nothing else does, in which case the product is missing a capability, and the question becomes how to build it well.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A mature productivity app has had flat weekly active users for six quarters, although the team has shipped many winning experiments. What would you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run more experiments and add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions why wins do not add up, looks at cohorts and segments, and considers larger moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Reconciles the sum of experiment lifts with the actual outcome, names the local-maximum risk, tests a new segment or job with a cheap bet, and sets expectations for the first quarter.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start with the puzzle in the prompt: many winning experiments and a flat result. They cannot both be fully true, so something is wrong in how the wins are counted, or the wins are being offset. <em>Reconcile first.</em> Suppose the team's experiments claim an added 9% of weekly actives over six quarters (invented), and the actual change is zero. I'd list the usual reasons. Novelty: lifts fade after a few weeks, and a test measured over two weeks overstates them. Overlap: two wins can improve the same users and not add. Shifts: a win in one place can reduce use elsewhere (cannibalization within the product). Proxies: experiments may be won on a metric (clicks on a feature) that is a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> for value and is not the number that matters. Offsets: other things are pulling the number down, such as older cohorts decaying faster or new-user cohorts getting smaller. I'd check each with data, using cohorts (C05): are older cohorts retaining as before, and are newer cohorts arriving at the same rate and activating as well? The experiment mechanics are C14. <em>What the answer is likely to be.</em> If the flat number is a balance between decay and acquisition, the improvements are keeping the line level and the real question is growth. If new cohorts are healthy and older ones decaying, the problem is long-term retention. If new cohorts are small, the product may be saturating its market. Each leads to a different move. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: remove what is hurting retention for older cohorts (for example, complexity added over six quarters). Add: a capability for a job the product does not serve today. Remove: features that few use and that make the product harder to learn. Reposition: a different segment or job. The local-maximum risk is real: a team that optimizes steps one at a time will not find a better position, so alongside the steady work I'd run one or two larger, cheap bets, such as interviews and a prototype for an adjacent segment, with a stated criterion for success, and I'd protect them from the weekly metric. <em>Expectations.</em> I'd tell leadership that the first quarter should produce a diagnosis and a small number of bets, not growth, and I'd agree what we would look at: the reconciled lift, cohort curves, and the results of the bets. The primary metric stays weekly actives for the core segment, with retention by cohort as an input, and a guardrail on the health of the core experience (task completion, support load). A counter metric is complexity: the number of features a new user must learn. <em>What would change my mind.</em> If reconciliation showed that the experiment wins were real and the offset was a one-off event such as a change in how users are counted, the answer would be a data fix and not a product move. If the larger bets failed on their criterion, I'd concentrate the team on retention for older cohorts.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A messaging app wants to grow in a country where many users have phones with very little free storage. What do you change?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Make the app smaller,&quot; with no reasoning about who is affected or how storage is lost.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies how the storage problem shows up for users, chooses a segment, offers different kinds of move and recommends one with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that received media, not the app, is the main consumer of storage, protects users who want to keep media, links storage to data cost, and states what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll state assumptions first, since I know this only in general terms: many phones have under about 1 GB of free storage, and these users are a sizable share of the target market. I'd verify that with device data on free storage at install and at uninstall. <em>Segment.</em> Users on phones with little free space. I choose them over users with ample storage because they are the ones losing the product. I give up users on high-end phones, who have no problem. <em>Problem.</em> A user's phone warns that storage is nearly full, and the easiest thing to delete is the largest app, or its media. The app is the first to go. Received photos and videos from large groups fill space quickly, and re-installing costs data. The workaround is the evidence: deleting the app and reinstalling later, or deleting media by hand. <em>The four moves.</em> Fix: make the app and its updates smaller (updates that send only what changed). Add: a storage manager that shows what uses space by chat and clears it in one tap. Remove: stop automatically downloading video over mobile data by default. Reposition: offer a &quot;lite&quot; version. I recommend the Add and the Remove together: a storage manager and a default that downloads media on request, which saves storage and data cost at once. The Fix is useful and smaller in effect, since the app is a minor share of what fills the phone. <em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: people who like to keep every photo must be able to, so media they star is never cleared automatically, and nothing is deleted without a clear choice. Ecosystem: media removed from the phone should remain retrievable from the sender or the cloud where the product offers it, so nothing is lost for good. <em>Size.</em> Assume 100,000,000 users and 30% storage-constrained: 30,000,000. Monthly uninstall rate is 12% for them against 4% for others (invented). If the change cuts the excess in half (4 points), that is 1,200,000 fewer uninstalls a month; with a 30% haircut, 840,000. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: 30-day retention among storage-constrained users. Input: the share who open the storage manager. Guardrails: media delivery success and the rate of &quot;media not found&quot; reports. Counter metric: the rate at which users complain about lost media, which would show that the default is too aggressive. I'd change my mind if the uninstall data showed that few exits followed a storage warning, in which case the cause is elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have two weeks and a small budget to learn whether people in a market you have never visited would use a savings product. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run an online survey and ask whether people would use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Studies today's workarounds, uses local researchers, observes behavior, and sets a decision rule before starting.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Corrects for biases specific to the setting, tests with a commitment and not an opinion, spots the licensing issue in handling people's money, and says what would stop the project.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd use the discovery plan (FW-C09-04). The decision this informs is whether to build a savings product for this market and for whom. I'd write the rule before I start: proceed if there is a widespread workaround that costs people something real, and if enough people show a behavior and not only an opinion. <em>Week 1: workarounds, proxies and fieldwork.</em> I'd hire a local researcher, since they know the language and norms and will be trusted. I'd expect informal savings (groups that pool money, a shopkeeper or relative who holds cash), and I'd ask about the last time a person set money aside, where it went and what went wrong. I'd also look at proxies: how people top up their phones tells me how they handle small sums, and how they send money tells me about trust and distance. I'd visit three places (a market, a workplace, a neighborhood), speak to about 12 people, and watch what they do, not only what they say. <em>Week 2: a cheap behavioral test.</em> A concierge test: with a licensed partner, because holding other people's money without a license is not something I can do informally, a local agent collects small deposits from 20 people for four weeks and records them in a notebook, with a simple receipt in the local language. The signal is deposits made, not interest expressed. <em>Biases to correct.</em> Politeness: people may say they would use it to please a visitor, which is why I ask about behavior. Interviewer effects: a local researcher who is not visibly senior. Sampling: an online survey or a phone survey would miss people without a phone, so I'd reach people in person. Translation: I'd have the questions translated and translated back. <em>Decision rule.</em> Proceed if at least 12 of the 20 deposit in at least three of the four weeks (invented), and if interviews show that today's workaround costs people through fees, loss or risk. Stop or reshape if people save but prefer the informal route for reasons of trust that a product cannot change. What I would not conclude: that the market &quot;is not ready.&quot; If the test fails I'd find the reason.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a feature for a video app for people whose connections are unreliable and whose data is expensive.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A lower-quality mode.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the binding constraint, ranks the pain points, offers different kinds of solution, and handles storage and data use.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Considers the effect on the people who make the videos, uses offline behavior as the primary design, protects users who want autoplay and quality, and sets guardrails on data use and storage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the existing app on mid-range phones, and a goal of more watching by people who now watch little or not at all because of cost and connection quality. Role: a core product that people choose for entertainment and learning. I know this only from public information. <em>Segment.</em> Viewers on a monthly data cap, on connections that drop, who commute or live where signal is poor. I give up viewers on home Wi-Fi, who have no issue. <em>Constraint stack.</em> Connectivity binds: cost and reliability. Devices follow: storage is limited, so downloads must be managed. Shared use and payment are secondary. <em>Pain points.</em> (1) A video buffers or fails mid-way and the data used is lost. (2) Autoplay and high default quality use the plan without the user choosing to. (3) The user cannot tell how much data a video will use. (4) Storage fills with downloads. I choose (1) to (3), which together are the data problem. <em>Solutions.</em> Assist: show the data used per hour and an estimate before playing. Automate: smart downloads of videos that the user is likely to watch, fetched on Wi-Fi overnight. Reframe: treat Wi-Fi as the place where the library is filled and mobile data as the exception. Remove: turn autoplay off on mobile data by default. Connect: let a friend share a downloaded video directly between phones. I recommend smart downloads with the data display and autoplay off on mobile data. They address the cause (data use at the wrong moment and place) and use an existing habit. <em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: those who like autoplay keep a setting, and quality stays high on Wi-Fi. Ecosystem: downloaded videos must expire and respect rights, and people who make videos earn from views, so offline views have to be counted and credited. Storage: downloads are capped and cleared with a simple rule, such as removing the oldest watched ones first. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: watch time per month among capped users. Input: the share of watch time served from downloads. Guardrails: megabytes used per watched hour, and the share of downloads that expire unwatched, which measures waste. Counter metric: uninstalls linked to a full storage warning. I'd change my mind if capped users said their main problem was the cost of the plan itself, in which case a carrier partnership is the first move. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts at Google often concern its products for users with limited connectivity <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A payments company wants to help small online merchants in a new country. How do you decide what to build first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Support the local currency and the most popular cards.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Ranks payment methods by the share of payments they carry in the target segment, considers the licensing lead time, and builds in phases.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats licensing, verification and data storage as part of the product, finds the revenue model the market allows, and says how to learn quickly while the license is pending.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume a company with a payments platform elsewhere, and a goal of a first cohort of merchants taking payments reliably. Country details are assumptions: I'd verify them with public data on payment methods and with interviews. <em>Segment.</em> Small merchants who sell through social media and chat and take orders without a website, over established stores that already integrate gateways. They have the most pain and the least service. I give up the developers and stores where our existing product fits, and I'd serve them with the same platform later. <em>What the market uses.</em> Assume, for the target segment's sales by value (invented): bank transfers through a local QR system 55%, cash on delivery 30%, cards 15%. These numbers drive the ranking. <em>Options.</em> | Option | Share of target sales covered | Time to market | Risk | |---|---|---|---| | A. Cards first (our existing capability) | 15% | Short: about two months | Low, and a small market | | B. The local bank-transfer method | 55% | Longer: about six months, with licensing | Medium: regulation and a new integration | | C. Cash-on-delivery support and reconciliation | 30% | Medium | Medium: the cash is collected by a third party | <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend B as the main build, since it carries the most sales, with the licensing and verification work started in week one because it has the longest lead time. In parallel I'd launch A to a small group of merchants who also sell abroad, to learn about onboarding, settlement and support, which would otherwise wait six months. C comes next, built as a reconciliation tool that matches courier-collected cash to orders. This gives up an early broad launch. <em>Beyond methods.</em> Merchant verification in local terms (a tiered process with lower limits at first, so honest sellers without formal papers are not excluded), data storage in the country if the rules require it, settlement timing in local business days, and a dashboard in the local language. Revenue: I'd check whether the local method carries a merchant fee by policy; if it does not, I'd price the software (reconciliation, invoices, reports) and earn on the minority of payments that allow a fee <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026: a fee applies from 15 October 2026 to merchant payments above 2,000 rupees]</code>. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of target merchants able to accept their most-used method, and the share of payment attempts that succeed, by method. Input: time from sign-up to first payment. Guardrails: fraud and dispute rates, and settlement delay. Counter metric: the share of merchants who keep using their old method in parallel after three months, which shows a gap. I'd change my mind if interviews showed that cash on delivery was the dominant pain, in which case C would move first. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe publishes documentation on local payment methods and <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts ask how to prioritize payment methods and compliance in a new country <code>[V: Stripe docs, Supported payment methods; the prompt descriptions remain candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or roadmap is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Launch a mobile wallet in a market where card use is low. (Use India as the worked case.)</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build a wallet where users can store money and pay at shops,&quot; with no analysis of what people use instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tests the premise by asking what people use in place of cards, identifies the rival, chooses a segment and designs around regulation and trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that a free interoperable rail changes what a wallet can offer, competes on what a device maker can uniquely provide (security, setup, integration across devices, privacy) and not on a new balance, and handles revenue, regulation and distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll test the premise first: &quot;card use is low&quot; does not mean people lack ways to pay. It means the way they pay is something else, and the answer depends on what. In India that is largely interoperable account-to-account transfers through the Unified Payments Interface (UPI), scanned from a QR code or approved in a payment app <code>Public</code> <code>[V: reference summaries of NPCI]</code>. Many different apps connect to the same rail, and merchant payments from bank accounts have carried no merchant fee by policy since 2020, with a 0.4% merchant discount rate (capped at ₹300) on merchant payments above ₹2,000 from 15 October 2026 <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026, as reproduced by FinTech BizNews]</code>. So the rival is not cards; it is free, interoperable and already habitual. A new wallet with a stored balance of its own has to beat that, and it has no natural advantage. <em>Goal and company.</em> I'll assume a device maker, so the setting is a phone, a watch and the services around them, and the goal is that the device becomes the way people pay, which strengthens the whole ecosystem. I know this only from public information, and nothing here describes any existing product. <em>Segment.</em> By behavior: urban smartphone owners who pay in shops several times a day; people who have a bank account but no card; people new to digital payments in smaller towns. I choose the first, because the frequency is high, they have capable devices and the habit is established, so the switching question is clear. I give up first-time digital payers, who need a different, assisted design, and I'd treat them as a later segment. <em>Constraint stack.</em> Payments bind: the dominant rail decides everything. Trust follows: people entrust money to an app, so the app's identity, security and dispute route matter. Regulation sets the order of work: payment intermediaries need authorization, and payment data must be stored in India (the Reserve Bank of India's 2018 directive) <code>Public</code> <code>[V: RBI FAQ, 2019; RBI payment aggregator directions, 2025]</code>, and general data-protection rules apply (the DPDP Act, in phases) <code>[V: law-firm summaries of the DPDP Rules]</code>. <em>Options.</em> | Option | Idea | Assessment | |---|---|---| | A. A closed wallet with its own balance | Users load money; pays within the wallet | Competes with free interoperable apps; needs licensing; no reason to switch | | B. A front end to the interoperable rail | The wallet lets users pay from their bank accounts through the rail, with the device's strengths | Fits the market; differentiates on trust, ease and security | | C. A pass wallet for tickets, transit and loyalty cards | Not payments; passes and identity | Lower regulatory burden; a smaller first step | | D. Stay out of payments | Focus on identity and passes | Safe and passive | <em>What a device maker adds.</em> On-device authentication (a fingerprint or face check), secure storage of credentials, a quick setup that finds the user's existing bank account, a tap or a scan that works the same on a phone and a watch, and a privacy stance (what stays on the device and what the company never sees). These are reasons to prefer the device's wallet that do not require a new balance. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend B with C: a wallet that connects to the interoperable rail through an authorized app provider and a partnership with banks, plus passes for transit and tickets as a second use. It gives up the control of a closed wallet and a revenue stream from float, and gains fit with how people pay. Revenue is indirect (a stronger ecosystem) and perhaps from services, since a fee per payment is limited on this rail (none up to 2,000 rupees; 0.4% above that from 15 October 2026) <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026, as reproduced by FinTech BizNews]</code>. Distribution: bank partnerships and merchant acceptance through QR codes that merchants already display. Next I'd add the watch and assisted setup for first-time users. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of eligible users who complete setup and make a first payment within seven days. Input: the share of setups that succeed on the first try. Guardrails: payment success rate, fraud losses and time to resolve a dispute. Counter metric: users who set the wallet up and go back to their previous payment app within a month, which shows whether it adds anything. <em>Risks and limits.</em> Regulation could delay the start, so authorization and data storage begin first. Trust: a failed payment with no clear dispute route loses a user for good. I would not launch without a dispute route and clear refund rules. I'd change my plan if research showed that the habit was so strong, and the free apps so good, that setup alone would not move people; then C becomes the product. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes a privacy position and designs hardware and software together, and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that wallet and payments prompts test local-rail understanding and privacy <code>[V: Apple privacy pages and integrated hardware, software and services; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any existing Apple product or its availability in any country.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a short-term credit feature for delivery workers whose income is irregular. How do you avoid harming them?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A quick loan with a clear interest rate,&quot; with no analysis of harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real need, designs a product that fits irregular income, and names the risk of a debt cycle with design responses.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets a written limit and a stop rule, measures repeat borrowing as a harm signal, protects data and consent, and notes the conflict of interest in a platform lending to its own workers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll start with the check (FW-C09-07), because for credit the harm question shapes the product. The user is described by what products lack for them: delivery workers who are paid per job, whose earnings vary week to week while costs (fuel, repairs, a phone bill) come on fixed dates. The need is bridging a gap of a few days. Existing options are informal lenders or loans with high fees and fixed monthly repayments that do not fit irregular income. <em>Design.</em> An advance against earnings already scheduled to arrive, repaid automatically from those earnings, with a flat, stated fee and no compounding interest. A cap: an advance may be at most a set share of average weekly earnings (say 30%, invented). No rollover: an advance cannot be extended or refinanced, and a new one cannot be taken until the last is repaid. The price is shown in the local language before the person agrees, as a single figure and in the effective annual cost, with a clear way to say no. Support is by voice as well as text. <em>Harm signals.</em> The main risk is a debt cycle: a worker takes an advance every week because the last one left a gap. I'd measure the repeat-advance rate, the share of users who take a new advance within seven days of repaying, and treat a high rate as a sign that the product is creating dependence, not bridging a gap. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of users who repay on time and do not take another advance within seven days. Guardrails: the effective cost of the fee, complaints, and the share of earnings taken by repayment. Counter metrics: the repeat-advance rate and the share of users who use it every week. Stop rule: if more than 25% of users take an advance in each of eight consecutive weeks (invented), I'd stop growth and redesign, since that pattern signals dependence. <em>Conflict of interest and data.</em> A platform that employs the worker and lends to the worker has more power than an ordinary lender. Repayment from earnings makes repayment safe for the company and can leave the worker with too little, so the repayment share is capped. The platform's earnings data is used to decide the limit, and the worker must understand and agree to that use; the data is not sold or used for other purposes. <em>Regulation.</em> Lending usually needs authorization and consumer-protection compliance, and details vary by country <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>, so I'd involve legal teams first or partner with a licensed lender. <em>My limit.</em> I would not launch credit with fees that trap people in repeated borrowing, with hidden or rolling charges, or with collection methods that shame or threaten, even if that version grew fastest. I'd change my plan if the first cohort showed that most advances were repaid and not repeated; I'd widen the cap carefully, and if the opposite, I'd not launch at scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your product succeeded in its home market and failed in the first country it entered. You have two weeks to write a post-mortem. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Conclude that the market was not ready and list lessons.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates assumptions from evidence, finds which dimension broke, uses funnel data and interviews, and proposes a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Compares the new market's funnel step by step with the home one, includes people who left, tests alternative explanations, sets criteria for another attempt or an exit, and avoids blame.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a diagnosis with a decision at the end, and I'd refuse the conclusion &quot;the market was not ready&quot; until the evidence earns it. The product has a funnel in both markets, so the first move is to compare them. <em>Week 1: evidence.</em> Day 1 and 2: funnel by step for the new market and the home market, split by device, connection and channel, to find where the new market loses people (acquisition, sign-up, first use, payment, return). Day 2 to 4: the six dimensions as a checklist of what we assumed against what was true: users, infrastructure, regulation, competition, distribution and price. Day 3 to 5: interviews with ten people who installed and left, and five who stayed; calls with the local team and any partners; support tickets by theme. I'd include the people who left, because the success stories are not where the answer is. <em>Example of what the evidence might show (invented).</em> | Assumption | Evidence | Verdict | |---|---|---| | Users have a personal phone | 8 of 12 interviewees shared a phone | Wrong; shared use | | Card payment works | 60% of payment attempts failed or were abandoned at the card step | Wrong; payments | | Paid ads will bring users at home-market cost | Cost per install was three times higher and retention was lower | Wrong; distribution | | Translation is enough | Support tickets about language were 5% of the total | Mostly right | <em>Alternative explanations.</em> A single cause is unusual. I'd ask whether launch timing, a competitor's move, or a technical fault (a payment provider outage) explains part of it, and whether the home market's success depended on conditions that do not exist here (for example, a network effect that needs density). <em>Week 2: synthesis and decision.</em> A short decision memo (FW-C02-06): what happened, which assumptions failed and what the evidence is, what we do not know, the options, and a recommendation. Options: iterate in the same market with specific fixes, narrow to one city or segment, change the model (a partner for distribution or a local payment method), or exit. For each, I'd write the condition that would make it right, such as &quot;continue only if payment success reaches 85% and week-four retention reaches 20% within a quarter (invented).&quot; <em>Blame and learning.</em> I'd write the post-mortem about decisions and information, not about people, and I'd hold a pre-mortem (FW-C02-05) for any second attempt: assume it failed in a year and ask why. I'd also record what worked, since a failed entry usually contains a part that did. I'd change my recommendation if the funnel showed that most of the loss happened before the product was even seen, in which case the question is distribution and not the product.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> We have a designer. What is the PM's role in UX?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The PM decides what to build and the designer makes it look nice.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Splits ownership of the problem and the solution, and names what the PM contributes.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives examples of problem framing versus solution dictating, mentions research, tradeoffs and decisions, and says how disagreements are settled.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd say the PM owns the problem and the outcome, and the designer owns the craft of the solution. My job is to make the problem clear: who the user is, what they are trying to do, what success looks like, what constraints we have (time, platform, technology, policy), and what we have learned from research and data. The designer's job is to turn that into a flow and an interface that works and feels right. The engineer tells us what can be built well. The difference shows in how I give input. &quot;Make the button blue and move it to the top&quot; hands the designer a solution and removes their craft. &quot;Forty percent of first-time users stall at the password step; I want 70% to finish, and we cannot add a screen&quot; gives them a problem and criteria, and they may find a better answer than I would have, such as showing the rules as people type. My other contributions: attending research sessions so that I see users struggle first-hand, making the tradeoffs and calls on scope, checking designs against the problem and not against taste, and bringing engineers in early. When we disagree, I'd propose a quick test with five users and let evidence decide before rank does. I'd keep the decision on what to build and the success criteria, and I would defer to the designer on how it should look and work unless it fails the problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A checkout screen shows &quot;Error 4021&quot; when a payment fails. What is wrong, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The message is ugly; make it friendlier.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the heuristic (help with errors), the cost to the user, and a better message.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Looks at the whole state (recovery, preserved data, support, tracking), prioritizes by impact and says how to measure the fix.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The principle is help with errors: an error should say, in plain words, what went wrong and what the person can do. &quot;Error 4021&quot; tells the user nothing, so I'd expect them to retry blindly, abandon, or contact support. At checkout, the cost is a lost order and, at a payment, a loss of trust: people worry whether they were charged. <em>A better message and the state around it.</em> &quot;Your card was declined. Check the number and expiry, try another card, or use a different payment method. You have not been charged.&quot; Plus: keep everything they typed and the cart intact, put the cursor at the field most likely wrong, offer alternative payment methods as buttons, and show a way to get help. The code can stay as a small reference for support. <em>Different failures need different messages.</em> A declined card, a network failure and a provider outage have different fixes. A single message for all of them is a smaller version of the same problem, so I'd ask engineering to map the codes to the main cases. <em>Priority and measurement.</em> This is a high-severity state because it sits at the revenue moment. I'd measure the share of failed payments that lead to a completed purchase within the session (recovery rate) and the support contacts about payment, and test the new message with five users shown a declined card. I'd also check accessibility: the message is announced by screen readers and does not rely on color alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> The deadline is in two weeks and the UX is rough. Do you ship?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship, we can fix it later,&quot; or &quot;No, quality comes first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets a quality bar by layer and by risk, and decides what to cut.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates accessibility, safety and honest information (not negotiable) from polish, records accepted rough edges with a date, and sets a measure and a rollback.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd decide by risk, using the quality dial (FW-C10-05): works, usable, delightful. I'd ask three things: what is the cost if a user fails here, how many users are affected, and what do we learn by shipping now? <em>Not negotiable.</em> The feature must work (correct, reliable), the core path must be usable, and accessibility basics (labels, contrast, targets) and honest information (clear pricing, clear errors) must be in place. These are not polish. States matter too: the empty and error states are where users get stuck, so they get at least a plain message and a next step. <em>Negotiable.</em> Animations and micro-interactions, secondary screens, visual refinement, edge-case flows that few people reach. I'd cut those first. <em>Make it explicit.</em> I'd write a short list of accepted rough edges with an owner and a date to fix them, since polish debt grows if no one tracks it. I'd release in stages (a small share of users first) with a guardrail on task completion and support contacts and a rollback ready, so a rough UX reaches few people before we learn how rough. <em>Decision.</em> &quot;Ship the core path with accessibility and states intact in two weeks to 10% of users; defer the animations and the settings polish to the next sprint, with a date.&quot; What would change my mind: if the rough area is a trust moment (payment, privacy) or the first-run experience, I'd hold the date, because failures there cost more than a late launch.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your designer wants a custom date picker for a signature feel. Your engineer wants the platform's standard one. What do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Go with the designer,&quot; or &quot;pick the cheaper one.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs user value, consistency, accessibility, cost and maintenance, and tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that standard components carry accessibility and familiarity, proposes a compromise (custom only where it matters), sets a test and a decision rule, and keeps the relationship in view.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd not decide by rank. I'd frame the question around the user and the cost. What task does the date picker serve, and is it a differentiator (for example, a travel app where date selection is the core action) or a supporting control (a birthday in settings)? <em>For standard:</em> people already know it (consistency and standards), it comes with accessibility support (screen reader labels, keyboard and voice operation), it is cheaper to build and maintain, and it updates with the platform. <em>For custom:</em> it can make a core task faster or clearer, and it expresses the brand. Its costs: building and testing for accessibility, more bugs, and upkeep across devices. <em>How I'd settle it.</em> A quick test: prototype both and watch five users pick a date range for the core task. Measure time to complete, errors and preference. If the custom picker is clearly better at the core task, and the team commits to accessibility and maintenance, it is justified. If not, the standard wins. <em>A compromise.</em> Use standard components for supporting controls and invest in a custom one only where the task is central. I'd record the decision and the reason so it is not reopened without new evidence. I'd tell the designer and engineer that both views are right about something: one is about the user's task and the brand, the other about cost, accessibility and maintenance. What would change my mind: a usability result that contradicts my expectation.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> The growth team wants a pre-checked box that opts users into marketing emails. They expect opt-in to more than double. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do it, since it doubles opt-in,&quot; or &quot;refuse&quot; with no reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Applies an ethical test, mentions the legal and trust risk, and proposes an alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies net outcomes (engaged recipients, complaints), proposes a better-timed ask, sets guardrails and says how to take the conversation to the growth team.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start by taking the growth team's goal seriously: they want more people who receive and value marketing messages. Then I'd run the ethical nudge test (FW-C10-06). A pre-checked box is not transparent (many people do not notice it), is easy to leave only if you spot it, is not something users would endorse if they understood, and is not symmetric. It is a dark pattern. In several jurisdictions, consent rules require an affirmative action to opt in, so it may also be unlawful <code>[V: GDPR Article 4(11) (clear affirmative action) and the ePrivacy Directive (opt-in for marketing email), as summarized in guidance]</code>, and I'd ask legal and privacy teams immediately. <em>Count net outcomes.</em> Suppose an unchecked box gets 18% opt-in and a pre-checked box 41% (invented). The pre-checked list is bigger, but its members did not choose it: say 9% of them open the emails, against 31% of the people who chose. Engaged recipients per 100 sign-ups: 18 × 0.31 = 5.6 versus 41 × 0.09 = 3.7. The honest approach produces more engaged recipients. The pre-checked list also generates more unsubscribes and spam complaints, which damage the reputation of our sending domain and lower delivery to everyone. <em>An alternative that serves the goal.</em> Ask at a better moment, with a clear value: after the first order, &quot;Want order updates and occasional offers? Yes / No thanks,&quot; unchecked, with an easy way to change it later. Test the wording and timing. Measure engaged recipients per 100 sign-ups, complaints and unsubscribes, with opt-in rate as a diagnostic and not the target (<a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05). <em>How I'd say it.</em> &quot;Opt-in is the wrong number. The right number is engaged subscribers, and the checkbox lowers it while raising legal risk. Let's test a better ask.&quot; What would change my mind: if the test showed that the honest ask gets too few opt-ins to matter, I'd look at the value we are offering, not at hiding the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Leadership wants to skip accessibility work to make the launch date. What do you say?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Accessibility is the law&quot; or &quot;okay, we'll do it later.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains the users affected, the cost of retrofitting, and a minimum bar.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the audience, separates launch-blocking items from later improvements, notes legal exposure and brand risk, offers a dated plan and a scope trade.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a scoping conversation with data. First, who is affected: people who use screen readers, voice control, larger text, captions or switch devices, and, by the inclusive-design framing, people with temporary and situational limits (a broken arm, bright sun, a noisy room). Suppose we estimate that 8% of our users use assistive features or larger text (invented), which is 80,000 of a million. That is not an edge case. <em>Cost.</em> Retrofitting accessibility after launch usually costs far more than building it in, because it touches structure (labels, focus order, contrast, targets) that is expensive to change once shipped. Skipping it also creates risk: legal exposure in some markets <code>[V: consent and children's data rules differ by country (GDPR, COPPA, India's DPDP Act)]</code>, app-store and partner requirements, and brand damage when people who cannot use the product say so. <em>A minimum bar that does not threaten the date.</em> Launch-blocking items: labels for every control and image for screen readers, contrast of 4.5:1 for normal text (a designer can check in minutes), touch targets of adequate size, keyboard and voice operation of the core path, and no information carried by color alone. These are small if built in from the start. Defer to a dated plan: enhancements to advanced features, deeper testing with assistive-technology users, and nonessential motion refinements. <em>What I'd offer.</em> &quot;We can hold the date if we protect the core-path basics, which add about three days, and defer the advanced items to a dated plan with an owner. If we skip the basics we will spend more later and risk exclusion on day one.&quot; I'd bring a trade: cut a non-core feature before cutting accessibility. What would change my mind: if the launch is a small, internal pilot with no public users, I'd allow a later bar for the pilot, and I'd write down the date by which it must meet the standard.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a usability test, two of five people failed a critical task. The team says five is too small a sample to act on. How do you respond?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Five is enough,&quot; or &quot;we need more users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains what the test is for (finding problems) and what it cannot do (sizing them).</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives the discovery argument and the interval, separates the existence of a problem from its rate, proposes analytics and a cheap retest, and decides on cost.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The team is right about one thing and wrong about another. A five-person test cannot tell us how common the failure is: two of five is 40%, but the 95% interval for that is roughly 12% to 77%, so the true rate could be small or large. We should not report &quot;40% of users fail.&quot; But the purpose of the test is to find problems, not to count them. If each participant finds about 31% of the problems on average (the commonly reported figure <code>[V: Nielsen and Landauer (1993): one user finds about 31 percent of problems]</code>), five participants find about 84% of them (1 − 0.69⁵). Seeing two people fail a critical task, and seeing why, is strong evidence that a problem exists and what causes it. The question is not whether it exists; it does. The question is how much it matters and how cheaply we can fix it. <em>What I'd do.</em> First, look at the cause: if both failures came from the same visible issue (a hidden control, an unclear label), the fix is probably cheap, and I'd fix it without waiting. Second, size it with analytics: what share of live users reach that step and abandon, and how does it compare with other steps? Third, run another round with five new participants after the fix, since people who failed now know the answer. <em>Decision.</em> If the fix costs a day, make it now. If it costs a month, size the problem first with analytics and weigh it against other work. I'd tell the team: &quot;Five people cannot give us a rate, and they have shown us a real problem and its cause. Let's fix the cheap part and measure the rest.&quot; What would change my mind: if the two failures had different causes, or if analytics showed almost no one reaches that step.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is your favorite Apple product, and what would you change?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Praise (&quot;it just works, the design is beautiful&quot;) and a minor wish (&quot;make it cheaper&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the job, why the product does it better than alternatives, and one change with evidence and a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Picks a specific, modest product, backs the strength with observation from use, makes the change modest and tied to a segment, names the tradeoff and the risk, and claims nothing about the company's internal reasons.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A composite candidate's answer; use your own product and experience.)</em> My pick is the wireless earbuds I use every day on my commute and for calls. The job: get audio into my ears with no effort, and switch between my phone and laptop without thinking about it. <em>Why it wins.</em> From my own use: pairing takes seconds when I open the case near my phone, and a spoken &quot;connected&quot; tells me it worked without looking at a screen. The case doubles as a charger, so I do not manage a cable. Those are strengths I'd protect: anything that adds a step to connecting would hurt the whole experience. <em>What I'd change.</em> One thing I observe: it is hard to know the case's battery level at a glance when the earbuds are in the case and the lid is closed, and I have been caught out on a long trip. I'd guess many commuters feel that, though I can't know how many. The problem: &quot;A commuter cannot tell whether the case has enough charge before leaving home, so they sometimes run out mid-trip.&quot; <em>Recommendation.</em> A small <em>add</em>: a battery reminder on the phone the night before, if the case is low and I have a regular early commute, which the phone could infer from my routine. Keep it opt-in, since an unwanted notification is a cost. Metric: the share of users who see a low-battery event during their commute, with a guardrail on how many switch the reminder off. Risk: noise, and use of routine data, which should stay on the device. <em>Tradeoff.</em> A reminder helps people with predictable routines and annoys the rest, so I'd make it a suggestion and test it with a small group first. What would change my mind: if few people actually run out of charge, the problem is not worth a feature. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that &quot;favorite product&quot; questions are common at Apple and reward specific, detail-aware answers <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product's behavior or the company's internal decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Pick the app you used most today and critique it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's great; I'd add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the goal and user, walks the flow, names strengths, ranks issues and recommends a change with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses observation from today's use as evidence, separates observation from inference, notes the strategic position briefly, and says what would change the view.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(Composite.)</em> The app I used most is a messaging app. I'll judge it as a way for me to stay in touch with a handful of people and a few busy group chats with little effort. I'll critique for a person like me: many groups, some of them active. <em>Flow.</em> Open, see the list of chats, open one, read, reply, return. <em>Strengths:</em> replying is fast, and message status (sent, read) is clear. I'd protect both. <em>Ranked issues (from today's use).</em> (1) Two group chats produced 80 messages while I was in meetings, and I could not tell which contained something addressed to me; I scrolled for several minutes (severity 4, reach high among people in busy groups). (2) Search found the message but opened the chat at the bottom, not at the message, so I scrolled to find the context (3). (3) Notification settings are per chat and buried (2). I'd rank the first at the top because it is the reason people mute groups, which weakens the product's core value. <em>Observation versus inference.</em> I observed the 80 messages and the scrolling. I infer that many people with busy groups feel this, and that muting is the response. I can't know the numbers. <em>Recommendation.</em> The problem: &quot;After time away, a member of a busy group cannot find what matters to them.&quot; A catch-up view that shows mentions and replies to me first, optional and in addition to the normal chat. Metric: the share of members of busy groups still active in the group after eight weeks, with a guardrail on messages sent per member (so the feature does not discourage posting). Risk: it reduces the visibility of messages not addressed to anyone, so I'd keep the full view as the default. Strategically, a messaging product depends on groups staying alive, which is why this issue matters to the business and not only to me. <em>Close.</em> &quot;It does quick replies well; its biggest problem for people in busy groups is finding what matters after time away. I'd add an optional catch-up view and measure retention in busy groups. I'd check it with five users and with the mute rate.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Critique the pairing and setup experience of wireless earbuds.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Make pairing easier and add more colors.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats it as a first-run experience across hardware and software, ranks issues, and recommends fixes with metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates what can be changed after shipping from what cannot, lets accessibility override the score, uses proxies for lagging outcomes such as returns, and avoids claims about any real product.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the category from use and from public information; nothing here describes a specific product. <em>Purpose and users.</em> The earbuds should take a new owner from the box to listening in about a minute and keep them listening. I'll critique for a first-time owner with a phone they already own, and note switchers as a second segment. The first-run experience is the first impression and the point where returns begin. <em>Flow.</em> Open the box, power on, pair, set up optional features (fit test, controls, assistant, companion app), first listening session. For a device there are extra surfaces: indicator lights, sounds and what the case shows. <em>Strengths.</em> Pairing that begins when the case opens near the phone (a pattern several makers use) is nearly effortless; a spoken &quot;connected&quot; gives eyes-free feedback. Protect both. <em>Ranked issues.</em> | Issue | Severity | Reach | Impact | Fixability | |---|---|---|---|---| | The fit test, which affects sound quality, sits deep in the app | 3 | 5 | 15 | 5 | | A failed pairing shows only a blinking light, with no message | 5 | 2 | 10 | 4 | | Connecting a second device is not obvious | 3 | 3 | 9 | 2 | | Case light colors are unexplained | 2 | 4 | 8 | 5 | | Setup guidance is visual only | 5 | 1 | 5 | 3 | By score, the fit test is first. The last row is an accessibility barrier: a low-vision owner cannot complete setup without help, so it is a requirement and goes on the list whatever its reach. <em>Hardware and software.</em> Some fixes live in the app and can be shipped any time (a fit-test prompt, a failure message, spoken guidance); others live in the device's firmware or hardware (light behavior, sounds) and may be fixed only before manufacture or by an update if the device supports it. I'd separate the lists and say which is which. <em>Recommendation.</em> The problem: &quot;A first-time owner does not find the fit test and gets no guidance when pairing fails, so some have poor sound and some return the product.&quot; Moves: an app prompt that offers the fit test right after connection (skippable), and a clear failure message with &quot;try again / reset&quot; plus a distinct sound. Metrics: primary, the share of new owners who reach a first listening session within five minutes; input, the share who complete the fit test; guardrails: pairing success, support contacts and returns in the first month; counter metric: the share who skip setup entirely. Returns arrive weeks later, so I'd use the five-minute measure as a proxy and check that it predicts fewer returns. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that critique prompts at Apple include device first-run experiences <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Critique a food-delivery app, from both the customer's and the business's point of view.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Fees are too high; add loyalty rewards.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Critiques the customer's flow and the business's economics, and notes where they conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the multi-sided tension (customers, restaurants, couriers), ranks issues for one segment, applies the strategic lens with numbers labeled as assumptions, and recommends with a guardrail on the other sides.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Customer view.</em> The goal: a household gets a hot meal on a weeknight with little effort and little uncertainty. The flow: choose a restaurant, build an order, pay, wait, receive. Strengths: browsing is easy and reordering a past meal is quick, which I'd protect. Ranked issues: (1) uncertainty during the wait: the estimate shifts and there is little explanation when it does (severity 4, reach high); (2) substitutions and missing items, handled after the fact (4, medium); (3) the total cost is clear only at the last step, where fees appear (3, high). I'd rank the first at the top because uncertainty is anxiety, and it drives support contacts. <em>Business view.</em> A delivery marketplace has three sides: customers, restaurants and couriers, and its economics depend on all of them. Suppose (invented) an average order of $30, a 25% commission, and $7 of courier and handling cost per order, leaving $0.50 contribution before marketing and support. That is thin, so each support contact (say $4) or discount matters. Distribution relies on promotions and advertising (inferred), and defensibility is limited because customers can use several apps. This explains why the company is sensitive to wait-time contacts and to fee transparency: both affect contact cost and conversion. <em>Where views conflict.</em> Showing the total cost early could lower conversion (people see the fee sooner) while raising satisfaction and reducing refunds. The honest design is to show it early, and measure net outcomes. <em>Recommendation.</em> Fix the wait: show an honest estimate with a range and a reason when it changes. Metric: delivery-related support contacts per 100 orders, with guardrails on conversion at checkout and on courier earnings (a more accurate estimate should not be achieved by pressuring couriers), and a counter metric on the share of customers who cancel after seeing a longer estimate. I'd label my numbers as assumptions and my view of the economics as inference.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A startup is launching a premium short-video service for phones, with expensive original shows, a subscription price of a few dollars a month, and no free tier. Critique the plan and say whether you would bet on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The content looks good, so it will do well,&quot; or &quot;it will fail because streaming is crowded.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Applies business model, distribution and defensibility, and names the key assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates what is within the team's control from what is not, lists the assumptions in order of fragility with ways to test them cheaply, and gives a decision with conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a strategic critique (FW-C11-04), and I'd label everything as based on the description, not on inside knowledge. <em>Purpose and moment.</em> The thesis is that people will pay for premium short video in pockets of time away from home. That is a context-dependent assumption: it depends on people having such moments (commutes, waiting) and on a phone being the screen they choose. If the context changes, the thesis weakens. A product that rests on one moment needs to work in others. <em>Business model.</em> A few dollars a month is thin against expensive originals. Suppose (invented) each show costs a few million dollars and the service needs several million paying subscribers to cover its content and operations. That needs large, fast growth, so the model's success depends on one number: paid conversion. A cheap test: pre-launch interest is not payment, so measure willingness to pay with a paid pilot. <em>Distribution.</em> A new standalone app has no audience. The short-video apps people already use are free and designed around sharing. If the product restricts sharing, it loses the main route by which free apps spread. So I'd ask how a person hears about a show and how they bring a friend. <em>Defensibility.</em> Exclusive content is expensive and can be matched by larger competitors; short episodes are easy to imitate; there is no network effect unless people share, and no switching cost. I'd ask what is hard to copy. <em>Assumptions in order of fragility, with tests.</em> (1) People will pay for short-form premium video: test with a paid pilot. (2) They will watch it in the moments assumed: observe a cohort. (3) Phone-only is enough: test with and without TV viewing. (4) Sharing is not needed: test with shareable clips. <em>Decision.</em> &quot;I wouldn't bet on the plan as described. I'd bet on a version that tests assumptions 1 to 4 cheaply before spending on a large content slate, and that works on more than a phone and lets people share. If a paid pilot shows strong conversion and retention, I'd scale.&quot; What would change my mind: strong evidence that the moment exists and that people pay.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You critique a checkout page and name its biggest issue. The interviewer says: &quot;I don't think that's the most important issue.&quot; What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Defends it stubbornly, or abandons it at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Restates the ranking criteria and evidence, listens, and revises if the interviewer's point is better.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the question of importance from the question of evidence, offers a way to settle it (a quick check), shows willingness to change the ranking without losing the structure, and stays collegial.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a request to show my reasoning, not as a verdict. &quot;That's a fair challenge. I ranked it first on three criteria: how bad it is when it happens, how many people reach it, and how fixable it is. The checkout page's biggest leak, as I saw it, is the hidden shipping cost at the last step, which I rated severity 4, reach high, because it affects everyone who gets that far and it comes as a surprise at the moment of payment.&quot; <em>Then ask.</em> &quot;Which issue would you rank first, and why? If you think the payment-method options matter more, that depends on how many people fail there. Do you have a sense of the numbers, or should I say how I'd find out?&quot; <em>Settle it with evidence.</em> &quot;The way to decide is the funnel: where do people leave the checkout, and what do recordings show at that step? If most leave at payment, I'd re-rank. If most leave when the shipping total appears, the ranking stands.&quot; I'd then say both versions of the recommendation in one sentence each. <em>Change my mind when warranted.</em> If the interviewer's point is better (for example, a trust problem at payment that affects fewer people but blocks purchase), I'd say so plainly: &quot;That's a good point; on severity it outweighs reach, so I'd move it up, and here is how that changes the recommendation.&quot; Changing the ranking in response to a better argument is a strength, provided the structure stays. <em>What I'd avoid.</em> Digging in because I said it first, or dropping my view with no reason because someone pushed. The goal is the best answer, and the criteria are what let us argue about it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-07</h3>\n";
